The Tsurugi is practically junk for a a samurai.  I'd gladly use the
starting katana over the Tsurugi so that I can #twoweapon.  I'd _much_
rather the Samurai have no Quest artifact at all, in fact, as the
nemesis with an instakill weapon requires quite careful management.

But #twoweaponing ordinary katana/longsword at +7 gives 1d10+1d8
+7+7+6+6 = something like 36 damage on average at Str 18/** with full
enchantment.  The Tsurugi gives something like 1d16+1d8+7+6+2 (you get
around 2 damage for expert I think), which is about 28 damage on
average.

The 5% instakill is a bit of a plus, but the 2-handed perils and -8
damage disadvantage outweigh it for me--and in practice as a lawful
I'm going to at least have Excalibur and a katana (adding another +5.5
damage to the #twoweapon damage)

Those numbers are just off the top of my head so they're likely off a
bit, but the basic point remains.

By the time I get the Tsurugi I almost always have Excalibur, and so
even with no Str or enchantment bonus I'm going to #twoweapon (I
certainly am not going to burn the skill slots getting the Tsurugi to
expert when it's not in the long-term plans, so it's
1d10+1d8+1d10=15.5 for twoweapon vs. 1d16+1d8 = 13 for Tsurugi, which
might make the bisect worth it but it's still close enough that I'll
stick with the long term plan and avoid the 2-handed weapon--every
point of Str damage adds another point to the #twoweapon damage
advantage)

The Staff is a bit harder to figure as level drain can be appealing
for reasons other than killing things, and is very hard to factor in
to kill ratios.



